## Abstract

Pediatric electrocardiograms (ECGs) differ substantially from adult recordings in heart rate, axis, interval durations, and amplitude characteristics, yet most existing ECG classification systems are trained on adult data and provide little or no insight into the rationale behind their predictions. We address both problems together by building a beat-level multi-lead ECG classifier for pediatric cardiovascular disease detection, organised around three complementary experts and a calibration-time fusion stage, and we audit the system with a five-method interpretability stack applied at four different granularities. The work uses the recently released ZZU pediatric ECG database, which provides {PLACEHOLDER: N} 12-lead recordings from {PLACEHOLDER: N} children aged 0–14, with disease diagnoses encoded against AHA/ACC/HRS and Chinese consensus standards [REF: Tan2025_ZZU]. We frame the task as 3-class beat classification (Normal/Non-cardiac, Structural Heart Disease, Arrhythmia & Electrical) under strict patient-disjoint 5-fold cross-validation. Three experts are combined: a 256-channel residual CNN with rhythm-aware cross-attention (V8), a wider 384-channel variant with test-time augmentation (V9), and an XGBoost classifier trained on 174 handcrafted beat-level features. The fusion stage is itself adaptive: on the per-fold calibration split we evaluate each individual expert, a weighted-grid combination, and a meta-logistic regressor stacked on expert probabilities together with per-expert entropy and confidence-margin features, then select whichever strategy performs best before applying it to the test split. Across five folds the fusion model achieved macro-F1 = {PLACEHOLDER: 0.XXX ± 0.XXX}, accuracy = {PLACEHOLDER: 0.XXX} and AUROC = {PLACEHOLDER: 0.XXX}, modestly outperforming the strongest single expert ({PLACEHOLDER: ΔF1 = +0.00XX}). Interpretability is provided through Integrated Gradients and GradCAM over the waveform branch, SHAP over the RR-feature branch, occlusion-based faithfulness validation, cross-attention weight extraction, and three fusion-level audit plots that explain *which* fusion strategy won, by *how much*, and *which expert* it leaned on per fold. We frame this work primarily as a methodological framework — patient-disjoint adaptive fusion with multi-granularity explainability — rather than as a leaderboard claim, and we are explicit throughout the paper about where the headroom over the strongest single expert is and is not statistically meaningful.

**Keywords:** Pediatric ECG, cardiovascular disease detection, deep learning, cross-attention, ensemble fusion, explainable AI, integrated gradients, GradCAM, SHAP, patient-disjoint cross-validation.

