## 1. Introduction

Cardiovascular disease remains the leading cause of mortality globally [REF: WHF2024]. While most adult cardiovascular conditions develop progressively over decades, there is now strong evidence that the underlying risk trajectory begins in childhood [REF: Li2003_Bogalusa; REF: Juonala2011]. Detecting pediatric cardiac pathology early — whether structural (congenital defects, cardiomyopathy) or electrical (arrhythmias, conduction disturbances) — meaningfully shapes long-term outcomes [REF: Zhu2022_JACCAsia].

The electrocardiogram is the most widely available and least invasive tool for this purpose. In adults, ECG-based deep learning has matured rapidly: end-to-end models now match or exceed cardiologist-level performance on selected tasks [REF: Hannun2019; REF: Ribeiro2020]. The pediatric setting is different in three concrete ways. First, the pediatric ECG is a moving target. Heart rate at birth routinely exceeds 140 bpm and falls toward adult ranges only by adolescence; QRS axis migrates from rightward (newborn) to leftward (older child); R/S amplitude ratios in the precordial leads invert with age [REF: Dickinson2005; REF: OConnor2008]. Second, the disease mix is different. Congenital heart defects, myocarditis, Kawasaki disease, and cardiomyopathy dominate the pediatric cardiac caseload; ischemic heart disease — the bulk of adult ECG literature — is rare [REF: Tan2025_ZZU]. Third, lead placement is often incomplete in young children, with V2/V4/V6 frequently substituted by V1/V3/V5 in patients under seven [REF: Chen2024_NatComm].

For these reasons, models trained on adult cohorts (PTB-XL [REF: Wagner2020], Shaoxing [REF: Zheng2020], SPH [REF: Liu2022_SPH]) cannot be assumed to transfer to children — and indeed, those datasets contain almost no pediatric records [REF: Tan2025_ZZU, Table 1]. Until very recently, the pediatric ECG community simply did not have a sufficiently large, disease-labelled corpus to train modern deep models. The ZZU pediatric ECG database (14,190 recordings from 11,643 children aged 0–14, with ICD-10–coded cardiovascular disease labels) is the first such corpus to be released openly at this scale [REF: Tan2025_ZZU].

The second gap is interpretability. Even where adult ECG models work well, the predictions are typically delivered as a single softmax vector; for clinical use, especially in pediatrics, that is not enough. Clinicians need to know *which part of the beat* drove a decision, *which leads* carried the signal, and — in ensemble systems — *which sub-model* contributed. Post-hoc attribution methods (Integrated Gradients [REF: Sundararajan2017], GradCAM [REF: Selvaraju2017], SHAP [REF: Lundberg2017]) and faithfulness checks via occlusion [REF: Zeiler2014] are now standard tools, but they are usually applied one at a time, to one model, in isolation. In a system that fuses heterogeneous experts, attribution at the input level only tells part of the story: the fusion decision itself needs to be auditable.

The contribution of this work sits at the intersection of these two gaps. We build a tri-expert beat-level classifier on the ZZU pediatric corpus, fuse the experts at calibration time using an adaptive strategy-selection procedure under strict patient-disjoint cross-validation, and audit the resulting system with explanations at four granularities: input waveform attribution, intermediate convolutional activations, rhythm-feature importance, and fusion-stage decision audit. We position the framework — particularly the patient-disjoint adaptive fusion — as the central methodological contribution; the headline accuracy numbers are competitive but the margin over the strongest single expert is modest, and we are explicit about that throughout.

The rest of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews adult and pediatric ECG modelling, ensemble fusion, and ECG-specific XAI, and identifies where the present work fits. Section 3 sets out the research gap, motivation, problem statement, objectives, and contribution list. Sections 4 and 5 describe the dataset and preprocessing pipeline. Section 6 develops the model architecture, including the cross-attention morpho-rhythm fusion. Section 7 describes the training procedure and the calibration-time fusion logic. Section 8 lays out the XAI stack. Sections 9–11 cover the experimental setup, evaluation metrics, and results. Sections 12–14 contain comparative analysis, explainability analysis, and error analysis. Section 15 discusses limitations and future work. Section 16 concludes.

