## 2. Literature Review / Related Work

### 2.1 Adult ECG classification with deep learning

Large adult ECG corpora — MIT-BIH [REF: Moody2001], PTB-XL [REF: Wagner2020], Shaoxing [REF: Zheng2020], SPH [REF: Liu2022_SPH], MIMIC-IV-ECG [REF: Brian2023] — have enabled a series of strong CNN- and transformer-based classifiers. Ribeiro et al. trained a 6-block residual CNN on 2.3 million 12-lead recordings and reached F1 scores above 0.80 across six rhythm classes [REF: Ribeiro2020]. Hannun et al. used a 34-layer 1-D CNN on single-lead recordings for 12-class rhythm detection at cardiologist level [REF: Hannun2019]. More recent architectures fold in transformer-style attention either across leads or across time [REF: AttnECG_paper_placeholder]. Across this literature the inputs are almost exclusively from adults, and the disease label set is rhythm-dominated.

### 2.2 Pediatric ECG modelling

Pediatric-specific deep ECG work is much sparser. The PHN dataset [REF: Saarel2018] (2,400 recordings of healthy North American children) and the FDA pediatric ECG warehouse [REF: FDA2021] (publicly inaccessible) have been used for normative range characterisation rather than disease classification. Chen et al. reported a congenital heart disease detector trained on a pediatric corpus, with attention paid to lead-availability constraints in young children [REF: Chen2024_NatComm]. The ZZU pediatric ECG database [REF: Tan2025_ZZU] is the first openly released pediatric dataset of sufficient size and with explicit ICD-10–coded disease labels for the conditions that dominate pediatric cardiology — congenital heart disease, myocarditis, cardiomyopathy, Kawasaki disease — making it the natural substrate for the present work.

### 2.3 Ensemble and fusion strategies for ECG

Ensembling has a long history in ECG competitions, dating back to the PhysioNet/CinC Challenge series [REF: CinC_overview_placeholder]. Strategies fall broadly into three families: late averaging of probabilities, learnt stacking via a small meta-classifier [REF: Wolpert1992_stacking], and gating mixture-of-experts approaches [REF: Jacobs1991_MoE]. Most published ECG ensembles fix the fusion rule a priori. Our work differs in that the fusion strategy itself is selected at calibration time per fold — see Section 7.4 for details.

### 2.4 Multi-modal architectures: morphology and rhythm

Several recent ECG classifiers separate the waveform branch from the inter-beat (RR) branch and then combine them. The standard combination is late concatenation followed by an MLP head [REF: WaveformRR_concat_placeholder]. A smaller body of work introduces cross-attention between the two branches at the record level [REF: TransformerECG_xattn_placeholder]. To our knowledge, cross-attention between morphology and rhythm specifically at the beat level — i.e., as part of a per-beat classifier rather than a windowed transformer — has not been systematically studied; we adopt this design and describe it in Section 6.2.

### 2.5 Explainable AI for ECG

Saliency-based methods have been applied to ECG models since at least 2019. Integrated Gradients [REF: Sundararajan2017] and gradient-times-input have been used for waveform-level attribution [REF: ECG_IG_placeholder]; GradCAM [REF: Selvaraju2017] is widely used to localise the QRS region [REF: ECG_GradCAM_placeholder]; SHAP [REF: Lundberg2017] is the standard tool for handcrafted-feature contributions [REF: ECG_SHAP_placeholder]. Occlusion-based methods provide a model-agnostic faithfulness check by direct intervention on the input signal [REF: Zeiler2014]. Most published ECG-XAI work applies one of these methods to one model. We argue in Section 8 that for a fused multi-expert system, a multi-granularity stack is more informative than any single method, and that the fusion stage itself needs an audit.

### 2.6 Where this work sits

Putting the threads together: pediatric ECG-specific deep models are rare, ensemble fusion is usually fixed rather than adaptive, and XAI is typically applied as a single method to a single model. We address all three within one framework, with the patient-disjoint adaptive fusion as the primary methodological contribution and the multi-granularity XAI audit as the secondary practical contribution.

