## 5. Data Preprocessing & ECG Signal Processing

The preprocessing pipeline is designed to produce, for each ECG record, a sequence of fixed-length beats with associated RR-interval features and 174-dimensional handcrafted features. The pipeline runs once offline; the model loaders read its outputs directly.

**Step 1 — Lead harmonisation.** For 9-lead records, V1/V3/V5 are used in place of V2/V4/V6 following the dataset's own convention [REF: Tan2025_ZZU]. All records are aligned to a canonical 12-channel layout.

**Step 2 — Filtering.** The MedEx MECG-300 acquisition device applies its own built-in filter [REF: Tan2025_ZZU]. We apply only a mild zero-phase 0.5–40 Hz Butterworth bandpass to standardise the spectrum across records, since record lengths vary from 5–120 s and a few records exhibit baseline wander not fully suppressed by the device filter.

**Step 3 — R-peak detection and beat segmentation.** R-peaks are detected on lead II using a Pan-Tompkins variant via NeuroKit2 [REF: Makowski2021_NeuroKit2]. We segment a 300-sample window around each R-peak: 100 samples (200 ms) pre-R, 200 samples (400 ms) post-R, at 500 Hz. The pre/post split is chosen so that the window covers the late portion of the P wave through the end of the T wave in typical pediatric heart-rate ranges (60–160 bpm). Beats whose window would run outside the record boundary are dropped.

**Step 4 — Per-beat tensor.** Each beat is stored as a `300 × 12` float32 array on disk in a single memory-mapped binary file (`beats.dat`), with an accompanying `beats_meta.npz` containing labels, record IDs, and an 8-dimensional RR-interval feature vector per beat. The RR features are: mean, std, coefficient of variation, min, max, RMSSD, heart rate, and an HR-variability composite. These are computed per-record and broadcast to every beat from that record.

**Step 5 — Handcrafted feature vector for the XGBoost expert.** A separate 174-dimensional feature vector is computed per beat, covering: per-lead morphological descriptors (R amplitude, S amplitude, QRS duration, ST level), per-lead T-wave shape statistics, fiducial-point ratios, cross-lead correlations, and the same RR-derived rhythm statistics. The full feature list is supplied in Supplementary Table S1.

**Step 6 — Standardisation.** RR features and handcrafted features are z-normalised using the training-split statistics of each fold (fit on train, applied to calib and test). Beats themselves are not z-normalised: scale information is informative for several of the target classes and the CNN handles it through batch normalisation.

**Step 7 — Patient-disjoint outer/inner splits.** This is the single most important design decision in the preprocessing pipeline. Beats from one patient are extremely correlated, so a beat-level random split inflates test performance dramatically [REF: Kachuee2018_leakage_placeholder]. We perform the split at the *record* level. Specifically, we use stratified shuffle splitting over unique record IDs to produce a 5-fold outer split (80/20 train/test on records) with class proportions matched on majority-class-per-record. Inside each outer training set we carve out a 15% inner calibration split (still record-disjoint from the outer test). Calibration is the set on which fusion-strategy selection happens; it is never seen by training or by final test reporting.

**Step 8 — Augmentation.** Training-time augmentation applied to beats only (not RR features): additive Gaussian noise (σ = 0.025, p = 0.7), per-record amplitude scaling (±15%, p = 0.6), random lead dropout (1–2 leads zeroed, p = 0.35), and circular time-shift (±15 samples, p = 0.4). These are deliberately mild, since beats already carry strong class signal and aggressive augmentation harmed validation F1 in early ablations.

