## 8. Explainable AI Methodology

We apply five interpretability methods at four granularities of the model, and three diagnostic plots at the fusion stage. The motivation for layering is twofold: (a) no single attribution method is unambiguously trustworthy [REF: Adebayo2018_sanity; REF: Kindermans2019_unreliability], so faithfulness checks across methods are valuable; (b) in a fused multi-expert system, input-level attributions only describe what one expert was looking at, not why the *fusion* made its choice.

### 8.1 XAI-1 — Integrated Gradients on the waveform

We compute Integrated Gradients [REF: Sundararajan2017] on the V9 expert (the strongest single waveform model) with respect to the 12-lead beat input, using a zero baseline and 50 integration steps. For each test-set sample we obtain a 12×300 attribution map. Per-class averaging gives a mean |IG| profile over time that can be overlaid against the canonical pediatric beat regions (P, QRS, ST/T). Per-lead heatmaps give the additional spatial dimension.

### 8.2 XAI-2 — GradCAM on the last conv block + IG/GradCAM agreement

GradCAM [REF: Selvaraju2017] is applied to the final convolutional layer of V9's morphology branch (`morph.s3.c2`, which has 384 output channels and a temporal extent of 75 samples — i.e., the time axis after two stride-2 downsamples). The class-conditional gradient-weighted feature map is averaged across channels, ReLU'd, and upsampled to the 300-sample input resolution. We then compute the Pearson correlation between the IG and GradCAM curves per class as a coarse agreement metric. High agreement is not by itself a guarantee of faithfulness, but persistent disagreement would have been a red flag.

### 8.3 XAI-3 — SHAP on the rhythm branch

We apply SHAP's GradientExplainer [REF: Lundberg2017] to the V9 expert with the morphology branch frozen, so the explainer only varies the 8-D RR-feature input. This isolates the contribution of rhythm features and gives a per-class Shapley value per feature. A background of 50 randomly drawn samples is used; we repeat over 5 different backgrounds and average to reduce variance. Per-class mean-|SHAP| bars and beeswarm plots are produced. The point of running SHAP only on the rhythm branch is that IG and GradCAM cover the waveform; SHAP fills in the part of the model they cannot easily attribute.

### 8.4 XAI-4 — Occlusion sensitivity (faithfulness)

A 40 ms zero-mask is slid across the beat in 20 ms steps. For each window position we record the drop in the V9-predicted probability of the true class. The resulting ΔP curve is a *direct* (non-gradient) faithfulness signal. We then compute (i) the overlap between the top-20% IG region and the top-20% occlusion region per class, and (ii) the Pearson r between the IG and occlusion curves. Both are reported in Table {PLACEHOLDER: T_faith}.

### 8.5 XAI-5 — Cross-attention weight extraction

Because the cross-attention block stores its softmax-over-heads weights α on the module after every forward pass, we can recover per-beat, per-class, per-head attention weights at zero additional cost. We aggregate by predicted class and head, giving a small 3-class × 2-head summary table. Clinically, the expected pattern would be that the arrhythmia-and-electrical class draws more strongly on the rhythm head than the structural-disease class. We report whether that pattern holds in Section 13.

### 8.6 V11-A, V11-B, V11-C — Fusion-stage audit

The three plots below are the part of the XAI stack that is specific to a *fused* system and that we believe is genuinely novel in this combination.

- **V11-A — winning strategy per fold.** A bar chart of which of the five candidate fusion strategies won the calibration competition on each fold. This is the most basic form of fusion audit: it answers "did we always pick the same rule, or did the adaptive step do real work?"
- **V11-B — Δ-vs-strongest-single-expert per fold.** Per-fold bars of (fusion F1 − strongest-single-expert F1). Positive bars indicate folds where the fusion meaningfully improved over the best single model; near-zero bars indicate folds where the fusion fell back to a single expert.
- **V11-C — expert-weight distribution.** On folds where weighted_grid won, the selected (w_v8, w_v9, w_xgb) triplet is plotted. This reveals which expert the fusion leaned on most strongly per fold.

We treat these three plots as the audit trail a clinical reviewer would need when asking "why does this system trust the rhythm branch more on fold 3 than on fold 1?"

