## 16. Discussion

We had three things in mind when we started this work: build a pediatric-specific classifier on a recently-released corpus, do the fusion in a way that does not pretend a single rule is best, and explain the resulting system at more than one level.

**On the fusion gain.** The headline ΔF1 is small. We were initially disappointed by this and spent some time chasing larger gains through grid refinement, alternative meta-features, and longer training. What we eventually realised is that V9 is already close to the ceiling on what a single-beat, single-expert model can extract from this corpus — the residual confusion is dominated by genuinely ambiguous cases (Section 15) rather than by anything a stronger combiner would fix. The contribution we are putting forward is therefore the *correctness* of the fusion procedure under patient-disjoint splits — not its raw lift.

**On the cross-attention.** The cross-attention block is not the part of the model that explains most of the variance in performance. Replacing it with simple concatenation in an ablation gave {PLACEHOLDER: ΔF1_ablation} on fold 1 alone. The contribution we put forward for this component is interpretive: the attention weights are extractable, class-stratified, and they make architectural sense. They are an explanation tool, not a performance miracle.

**On the XAI stack.** The multi-method approach mattered. Looking at IG alone, we would have come away with a clean QRS-centric story. Looking at IG + occlusion, we noticed that Structural-class decisions are made out of more diffuse signal than the IG maps alone suggested. Looking at the fusion-stage audit, we noticed that the calibration-time strategy switch between folds 1–2 and 3–5 is not noise — it reflects something stable about how the experts disagree on different patient subsets.

**On pediatric vs. adult ECG.** The adult-pretrained baselines in Section 12 made the case for pediatric-specific modelling concretely: pretraining transfers some, but not all, of the relevant features, and a from-scratch pediatric model outperforms an adult-pretrained one after fine-tuning. This is not surprising — the physiology argues for it — but it is good to have on the record empirically.

**What surprised us.** Two things. First, how strongly the Structural class confused with Normal compared to the (clinically more obvious) Structural ↔ Arrhythmia confusion: this turned out to track the dataset's own observation that disease-labelled records can have normal-looking ECGs. Second, how the XGBoost expert, despite being the weakest single model by F1, was never given zero weight by the calibration-time fusion. Its contribution is small but consistent — its handcrafted features carry a slightly different signal than the CNN's learnt features, and the fusion finds it useful even when it cannot stand alone.

