## 17. Limitations

We are explicit about what this work does *not* establish.

- **Single-site cohort.** All records come from one hospital. Acquisition device, lead-placement convention, and patient demographic mix are correlated. External validation on a second pediatric cohort is the most important next step.
- **No prospective evaluation.** We report 5-fold cross-validation on a static corpus. The system has not been deployed prospectively, and we make no claim about real-time decision-support utility beyond what the OOF metrics support.
- **Three-class collapse loses within-class detail.** Collapsing 19 ICD-10 categories into three discards clinically meaningful distinctions (e.g., between fulminant and viral myocarditis, both encoded I40.0 in the dataset). A finer-grained classifier with the same fusion framework is a natural extension.
- **Beat-level classification, not record-level.** We classify individual beats and aggregate. Record-level decisions need an additional aggregation step (majority voting, average pooling) that we describe but do not benchmark exhaustively.
- **9-lead records.** 1,856 records use a 9-lead montage with V1/V3/V5 substituted for V2/V4/V6. The model is trained on the canonical 12-channel layout with the substituted leads in their assigned channel slots; we do not separately benchmark 9-lead-only performance.
- **Modest fusion gain.** As emphasised above, ΔF1 over the strongest single expert is small. The fusion framework is correct and the procedure is interpretable; it is not a magic accuracy lever.
- **XAI methods inherit their own limitations.** IG, GradCAM, and SHAP all rely on assumptions that have been shown to fail in adversarial regimes [REF: Adebayo2018_sanity; REF: Kindermans2019_unreliability]. We use multiple methods together precisely because no single one is unambiguously trustworthy.
- **Statistical power on 5 folds.** With n = 5 fold-level F1 estimates, formal significance testing of small differences is underpowered. We report mean ± std and per-fold breakdowns rather than p-values.
- **No human-reader study.** The XAI outputs have not been evaluated against cardiologist judgement on the same cases. Whether the explanations the model produces are *useful* to a reader, beyond being faithful to the model, is a separate question this paper does not answer.

