## 19. Ethical Considerations

The ZZU pediatric ECG database was approved for public release by the Ethics Committee for Scientific Research and Clinical Trials at the First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University (2024-KY-0221-003 and 2025-KY-0369-001), with informed consent waived for de-identified scientific reuse [REF: Tan2025_ZZU]. No additional identifiable data were collected for this study.

The system is intended as decision support, not as an autonomous diagnostic tool. The error analysis (Section 15) shows that the most clinically consequential error type — false negatives on Structural disease — has a non-trivial rate, often on records that the original clinical reading also labelled as ECG-normal. Any deployment in a clinical workflow would need (a) a human-in-the-loop review step, (b) an external validation cohort, and (c) explicit calibration of the false-negative rate against the downstream clinical pathway.

We note one structural concern with pediatric AI more broadly: pediatric cohorts are smaller and more demographically homogeneous than adult cohorts, so models trained on them risk encoding cohort-specific biases more strongly. The ZZU corpus is geographically and ethnically concentrated; transfer to other populations should be validated before use.

---

## 20. Conclusion

We built a tri-expert beat-level classifier for pediatric cardiovascular disease detection on the ZZU pediatric ECG corpus, organised around (i) two waveform CNNs of different capacity with beat-level cross-attention between morphology and rhythm, (ii) an XGBoost expert on 174 handcrafted features, and (iii) a calibration-time adaptive fusion stage that selects between per-expert pass-through, weighted-grid combination, and meta-logistic stacking on a per-fold basis under patient-disjoint cross-validation. The fusion improved macro-F1 by {PLACEHOLDER: ΔF1} over the strongest single expert — a modest gain that we frame as evidence for the *correctness* of the fusion framework rather than as a leaderboard claim. We audited the resulting system with explanations at four granularities: input waveform attribution (IG, occlusion), intermediate feature maps (GradCAM), rhythm-feature importance (SHAP), and the fusion stage itself (winning strategy, per-fold gain, expert-weight distribution). The clinical interpretation of the explanations is consistent with the physiological priors we would expect from pediatric ECG, and the error analysis is consistent with the dataset's own documentation of disease-positive records carrying normal-looking ECGs. The system is positioned as decision support, the limitations are stated explicitly, and the next step is external validation on a second pediatric cohort.

